# scoursh redaction rules - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.3
#
# Applied by redact() in lib/findings.sh before ANY evidence, log line, or
# report byte is written (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 9).  Each match is replaced
# with <redacted:KIND:DDDDDDDD>, where DDDDDDDD is the first 8 hex characters of
# the SHA-256 of the raw matched bytes, so a reader can tell two distinct
# secrets apart and recognise the same secret across three findings without the
# secret being present.
#
# Adding a new secret shape is a data change, per docs/DESIGN.md §16.
#
# AUTHORING NOTE.  These patterns are applied to in-memory strings, but through
# the same engine wrapper the scanner uses, so the whole §8.2 portable ERE
# subset is available exactly as it is for a pattern rule.  What is NOT
# available is bash's own `=~`: the system regcomp on macOS/BSD supports none of
# \b, \w, \s or \d (measured), which is why redact() pipes through the engine
# rather than matching in-process.
#
# MATCHING IS LINE-ORIENTED (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §8.2): a pattern can never
# match across a newline.  A multi-line secret therefore needs one rule per line
# SHAPE, not one rule that tries to swallow the block.
#
# That is why the PEM private key is two rules.  It was one, titled "header and
# body", whose `[^-]*` tail was written to swallow the body - and could not,
# because the matcher sees one line at a time.  On a real (multi-line) key only
# the header was replaced and every base64 body line was written verbatim into
# findings.json, findings.jsonl, report.md and report.html.  The test that
# should have caught it asserted a single-line pseudo-PEM, a shape no real key
# has, where the correct and the broken implementation agree.
#
# The body rule is deliberately a little greedy: a line that is entirely base64
# and at least twenty characters long, or any base64 line carrying `=` padding.
# Over-redaction costs a reader some evidence; under-redaction discloses a
# credential, so the asymmetry decides the trade.

id: SAST-REDACT-AWS_SECRET-01
format-version: 1
title: AWS secret access key assignment
pattern: (aws_secret_access_key|AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY|aws-secret-access-key|awsSecretAccessKey)[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*["']?[A-Za-z0-9/+=]{40}
dialect: ere
kind: AWS_SECRET

id: SAST-REDACT-PRIVATE_KEY-01
title: PEM private-key header line
pattern: -----BEGIN [A-Z0-9 ]*PRIVATE KEY-----
dialect: ere
kind: PRIVATE_KEY

id: SAST-REDACT-PRIVATE_KEY_BODY-01
title: PEM private-key body line
pattern: ^([A-Za-z0-9+/]{20,}={0,2}|[A-Za-z0-9+/]{2,}={1,2})$
dialect: ere
kind: PRIVATE_KEY

id: SAST-REDACT-JWT-01
title: JSON Web Token
pattern: eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_=-]+\.eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_=-]+\.[A-Za-z0-9_=-]+
dialect: ere
kind: JWT

id: SAST-REDACT-BEARER-01
title: Bearer or API-key credential in a header or assignment
pattern: ([Bb]earer|[Aa]uthorization:|api[_-]?[Kk]ey[[:space:]]*[=:])[[:space:]]*["']?[A-Za-z0-9._~+/=-]{16,}
dialect: ere
kind: BEARER

# The two rules below close the gap that let a matched credential reach every
# report format in the clear.  They are the SECOND of two layers and neither
# subsumes the other: lib/findings.sh's `finding_set_secret_match` guarantees
# that a SECRETS CHECK never writes its own match, whatever this file contains,
# while these rules mask a credential that turns up INCIDENTALLY - in some other
# check's evidence, in a crawled URL's query string, in a log line, or in an
# adapter-supplied title or remediation - which the provenance layer cannot see.
#
# Adding these alone would have been the wrong fix, and adding neither leaves a
# real hole; the two answer different questions.
#
# Case is spelled out rather than flagged, per §8.2: matching is case-sensitive
# and `(?i)` is not in the portable subset.  The three spellings enumerated are
# the ones that actually occur - all-lower, Capitalised, ALL-CAPS - because a
# per-letter `[Pp][Aa]...` class costs legibility for shapes nobody writes.

id: SAST-REDACT-PASSWORD-01
title: Password literal assignment
pattern: (password|Password|PASSWORD|passwd|Passwd|PASSWD|pwd|Pwd|PWD)[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*["'][^"'[:space:]]{6,}["']
dialect: ere
kind: PASSWORD

id: SAST-REDACT-API_KEY-01
title: API-key literal assignment
pattern: (api[_-]?key|Api[_-]?Key|API[_-]?KEY|apiKey|apikey)[[:space:]]*[=:][[:space:]]*["'][^"'[:space:]]{8,}["']
dialect: ere
kind: API_KEY
